            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "The growing amount of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2) is a strong agent of change, which indirectly impacts insects by altering the biochemical makeup of the host plants that insects feed on. Moreover, the increasing rate and severity of extreme weather events, for example, droughts, floods, and out-of-season storms, may result in abrupt and disastrous disturbances to insect life cycles and the ecological channels within which they dwell. It is against such a background of enhanced uncertainty and risk that the paradigms of conventional pest management are emerging as increasingly inadequate. The use of calendar spray plans and wide-spectrum application of pesticides is not only inefficient but also contributes to the intensification of the problem through the damage of non-target organisms, the rapid development of resistance to pesticide ingredients, and the disturbance of the ecological balance [3].",
